+# Global Fedoryuk connection and conditional quantization
+
+## Audited starting data
+
+After rotating the drive phase so that (F=|F|>0), define
+
+\[
+ y=z/\eta^{1/3},\qquad \varepsilon=\eta^{-2/3},\qquad
+ \eta=|F|/V,\qquad \delta=\hbar\omega _0/V,
+ \qquad e=E/(V\eta^{4/3}).
+\]
+
+The exact Liouville gauge and direct Fedoryuk equation are
+
+\[
+ \Psi=z^{-\delta}e^{\eta/z}u,qquad
+ \varepsilon^2u_{yy}=(Q_0+\varepsilon Q_1+\varepsilon^2Q_2)u,
+\]
+\[
+ Q_0={P(y;e)\over y^4},\quad Q_1={2(\delta-1)\over y^3},
+ \quad Q_2={\delta(\delta-1)\over y^2},\qquad
+ P(y;e)=1+2ey^2-2y^3.
+\]
+
+At (e_0=-3/2),
+
+\[
+ P(y;e_0)=-(y+1)^2(2y-1).
+\]
+
+Thus (y=-1) is double and (y=1/2) is simple.  If
+(e=e_0+\varepsilon e_1+O(\varepsilon^2)), the three roots of (P) are
+
+\[
+ y_\pm=-1\pm\sqrt{-2e_1/3}\,\varepsilon^{1/2}
+          +{4e_1\over9}\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^{3/2}),\qquad
+ y_s={1\over2}+{e_1\over9}\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^2).
+\]
+
+The local variables are
+
+\[
+ X={y+1\over\sqrt\varepsilon}=z+\eta^{1/3},\qquad
+ s=3^{1/4}X,qquad t=-is,
+\]
+
+and the two opposite-sector Weber problems give
+
+\[
+ e_{1,n}^{(R)}=\delta-1-\sqrt3(n+1/2),\qquad
+ e_{1,n}^{(I)}=\delta-1+\sqrt3(n+1/2).
+\]
+
+The imaginary-axis branch, not the real-axis branch, has the sign found by
+the independent displaced/Bogoliubov calculation.  This agreement is not a
+global orientation proof.  At the end of stage 09 the
+**Fedoryuk--Bargmann orientation hypothesis remained unproved**: no domain
+chain had identified the compensated origin germ with the imaginary
+opposite-sector problem.
+
+All displayed scales and signs were rederived by expanding the exact
+coefficient, rather than copied.  In particular the splitting equation is
+(3X^2+2e_1=0), the rotation (t=-is) reverses the Weber-index relation,
+and a positive circuit about zero multiplies (z^{-\delta}) by
+(e^{-2\pi i\delta}).
+
+## Source audit and applicability table
+
+The source is M. V. Fedoryuk, *Asymptotic Analysis: Linear Ordinary
+Differential Equations*, Springer, Berlin, 1993, translated by Andrew Rodick,
+ISBN 978-3-540-54810-2, DOI 10.1007/978-3-642-58016-1.  The accessible source
+material confirms Chapter III, Section 2, “WKB Bounds in the Complex Plane”
+(from p. 87), Section 4, “Equations with Entire or Meromorphic Coefficients”
+(from p. 108), and Chapter IV, Sections 2, 4, 6, and 7 (simple complex,
+multiple complex, and two close turning points; the last starts on p. 211).
+It does not expose theorem numbers or complete theorem statements.  No
+number is therefore invented.
+
+The faithful limited paraphrase is: on a canonical complex domain carrying
+a single-valued action and suitable progressive paths, solutions of a
+large-parameter second-order equation admit WKB representations with error
+bounds; separate local constructions treat a simple, multiple, or pair of
+close turning points.  These local results assume the required domains and
+do not, by citation alone, construct a global chain through other turning
+points or a pole.
+
+| hypothesis | Kerr verification | status |
+|---|---|---|
+| large parameter | (\varepsilon^{-1}=\eta^{2/3}\to\infty) | verified |
+| meromorphic coefficient | (Q_0,Q_1,Q_2) are rational | verified |
+| nonvanishing leading term on a WKB domain | remove all roots and (0) | verified locally |
+| single-valued momentum/action | choose a sheet on a simply connected cut domain | verified locally |
+| simple turning point | (y_s=1/2+O(\varepsilon)), (Q_0'=-72) at criticality | verified locally |
+| close pair | (y_\pm+1=O(\varepsilon^{1/2})) | verified locally |
+| analyticity near the close pair | the pole and simple point lie outside a fixed disk about (-1) | verified |
+| progressive paths | can be chosen inside deliberately local sectors | verified locally only |
+| finite global chain with uniform overlaps | must join two origin sectors through Weber and possibly Airy domains | not verified |
+| passage at the fourth-order pole | the cited turning-point results do not cross (y=0) | not supplied |
+| uniform error bounds on every overlap | no complete collection of overlaps exists | not verified |
+| sheet and gauge monodromy | necessary reciprocal multiplier is known, but continuation has not been constructed | not verified globally |
+| identification with the entire Bargmann germ | requires cancellation of the essential and algebraic factors | not verified globally |
+
+No checked Fedoryuk theorem has the combined scope “fourth-order pole +
+close pair + remaining simple point + multivalued gauge + Bargmann endpoint”.
+This is the first decisive obstruction.
+
+## Momentum surface and candidate geometry
+
+Let
+
+\[
+ \mathcal R_e=\{(y,w):w^2=P(y;e)\},\qquad p=w/y^2.
+\]
+
+For three simple roots this is the two-sheeted genus-one compact cover
+branched at the three finite roots and infinity.  At criticality two branch
+points merge and the cover degenerates; the node corresponds to the double
+turning point.  The pole (y=0) is not a branch point of (p), because its
+order is even.  Choose the sheet with (p>0) just to the right of zero and
+cuts joining the close pair and the remaining root to infinity.  Actions
+(S_j=\int_{y_j}^yp,dy) are single-valued only after these choices.
+
+In the convention used here, Stokes curves satisfy (\Re S_j=0),
+anti-Stokes curves satisfy (\Im S_j=0), and boundaries separating
+canonical regions are separatrysy.  At the double point the Stokes
+directions are (\pi/4+k\pi/2), while the anti-Stokes directions are
+(k\pi/2).  At (y=1/2), (Q_0'=-72) and each family has three rays.
+
+A candidate finite chain has: a punctured origin sector carrying the
+compensated branch; an ordinary WKB overlap; a fixed close-pair Weber disk;
+another WKB overlap; an Airy disk if topology forces passage by (y_s); and
+a sheet-related origin/outer sector.  Numerical trajectories in the existing
+figure are useful evidence for this inventory, but do not establish
+univalence, progressive paths, or constants uniform in (\varepsilon).
+Both infinity exponentials have Bargmann-admissible order-one-half growth,
+so discarding one of them is not a spectral boundary condition.
+
+## Local connection matrix and the missing global matrix
+
+Use (D_\nu(Z)=U(-\nu-1/2,Z)).  DLMF 12.2.19 gives, for the displayed
+orientation,
+
+\[
+ D_\nu(Z)=e^{-i\pi\nu}D_\nu(-Z)
+ +c_W(\nu)D_{-\nu-1}(iZ),
+\quad
+ c_W(\nu)={\sqrt{2\pi}\,e^{-i\pi(\nu+1)/2}\over\Gamma(-\nu)}.
+\]
+
+With the reciprocal normalization of the second basis vector this is
+represented by
+
+\[
+ M_W(\nu)=
+ \begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\pi\nu}&c_W(\nu)\\0&e^{i\pi\nu}\end{pmatrix},
+ \qquad\det M_W=1.
+\]
+
+Reversing the path or square-root sheet conjugates the indicated rotations
+and changes the corresponding phases; it does not move the zeros
+(c_W(n)=0).  An Airy transition, if required, must be inserted with the
+orientation fixed by the actual path.  Since that path is not proved, no
+specific Airy multiplier is asserted.
+
+In one transported WKB basis the only honest present global scheme is
+
+\[
+ M_{\rm glob}=B_{\rm out}\,M_A^{\chi_A}\,B_2\,M_W(\nu)\,B_1\,G_0,
+ \qquad \chi_A\in\{0,1\}.
+\]
+
+Here (B_1,B_2,B_{\rm out}) are the branch- and path-dependent overlap
+matrices, (M_A) is present only if the simple point is crossed, and (G_0)
+includes the Bargmann gauge.  The positive origin loop has
+
+\[
+ G_{z^{-\delta}}=e^{-2\pi i\delta},\qquad
+ G_u=e^{2\pi i\delta},\qquad G_{z^{-\delta}}G_u=1.
+\]
+
+The reciprocal multiplier is necessary for single-valuedness, but its
+realization by the unconstructed continuation is precisely part of the gap.
+Assigning numerical values to the (B_j) would disguise, not solve, it.
+
+## Minimal Fedoryuk--Bargmann connection lemma
+
+**Lemma (minimal, presently unproved).**  Fix (n) and let (\delta) range
+over a compact real set.  For sufficiently small (\varepsilon>0), there is
+a finite chain of the domains listed above, with a number of domains and
+overlap constants independent of (\varepsilon), such that:
+
+1. its initial canonical solution is exactly the sectorial continuation of
+   the normalized analytic Bargmann germ after gauge compensation;
+2. its close-pair map selects (t=-is), not the real-axis pair;
+3. every WKB--Weber and, if needed, WKB--Airy transition has a uniform
+   asymptotic matrix and the product realizes (G_u=e^{2\pi i\delta});
+4. the inadmissible coefficient at the final origin sector is a scalar
+   analytic function
+   \[
+   \mathcal C_{\rm Fed}=A(E,\varepsilon,\delta)
+       \{\Gamma[-\nu_{\rm eff}(E,\varepsilon,\delta)]^{-1}
+       +O(\varepsilon^3)\},
+   \]
+   uniformly near the (n)-th zero, with (A) bounded away from zero and
+   \[
+   \nu_{\rm eff}=n\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+   e=-\frac32+\varepsilon[\delta-1+\sqrt3(n+1/2)]
+   +\varepsilon^2\left[{\delta(1-2\delta)\over6}
+   -{6n^2+6n+1\over72}\right]+O(\varepsilon^3);
+   \]
+5. (\mathcal C_{\rm Fed}=0) is equivalent to absence of the enhanced
+   essential branch, hence to the exact Bargmann entireness condition in
+   this neighbourhood.
+
+The local Fedoryuk/Weber theorems supply neither items 1--3 nor the global
+uniform remainder and equivalence in items 4--5.  The gap therefore concerns
+the geometry of canonical domains, pole endpoint, uniformization overlaps,
+monodromy, error estimates, and identification of the Bargmann solution; it
+is one coupled global lemma rather than an additional local coefficient.
+
+Assuming the lemma, the simple zeros of (1/\Gamma(-\nu)) and an implicit
+function (or Rouché) argument give
+
+\[
+ \mathcal C_{\rm Fed}(E,\eta,\delta)=0
+\]
+
+and select the imaginary-axis branch.  Since
+(E/V=\eta^{4/3}e) and (\varepsilon=\eta^{-2/3}),
+
+\[
+ {E_n\over V}=-{3\over2}\eta^{4/3}
+ +[\delta+\sqrt3(n+1/2)-1]\eta^{2/3}
+ +{\delta(1-2\delta)\over6}-{6n^2+6n+1\over72}
+ +O(\eta^{-2/3}).
+\]
+
+This implication is proved; the lemma itself is not.  The known coefficient
+(K_n(\delta)\eta^{-2/3}) remains a formal Rayleigh--Schrödinger result and
+is not promoted to a Fedoryuk consequence.
+
+## Comparison and verdict
+
+| statement | leading | (\eta^{2/3}) | constant | (\eta^{-2/3}) | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| Rayleigh--Schrödinger | agrees | agrees | agrees | (K_n(\delta)) known | formal fixed-(n) expansion |
+| Bogoliubov | agrees | positive (\sqrt3) sign | incomplete without interactions | — | independent consistency check |
+| Olver | agrees | same conditional Weber zero | not globally derived | — | same global obstruction |
+| local Fedoryuk--Weber | agrees | both signs; imaginary agrees | agrees for both orientations | not computed here | controlled locally on compact scaled sets |
+| global Fedoryuk spectrum | leading double-root geometry only | conditional | conditional | not claimed | minimal lemma missing |
+
+The verdict is **CONDITIONAL REDUCTION PROVED**.  The shortest realistic
+proof route is to construct the critical graph on the momentum surface,
+cover one physical continuation cycle by finitely many fixed domains,
+derive pole-end Volterra estimates for the compensated solution, use the
+existing Weber and Airy parametrices on overlaps, and multiply their
+Wronskian-normalized transition matrices while tracking the logarithm.  A
+uniform (O(\varepsilon^3)) scalar remainder near a fixed simple Weber zero
+then suffices for the stated energy through order one.
+
+The symbolic verifier `scripts/10_verify_fedoryuk_global_reduction.py`
+checks the turning-point expansions, both Weber signs, the displayed matrix
+and determinant, its integer zeros, gauge-monodromy cancellation, and the
+conditional energy algebra.  Its tests are regression checks, not numerical
+evidence for the missing global lemma.
