+We can nevertheless state the exact reduction furnished by the local
+calculation.  In the convention
+\(D_\nu(Z)=U(-\nu-1/2,Z)\), the oriented Weber connection formula has
+coefficient
+\begin{equation}
+ c_W(\nu)=\frac{\sqrt{2\pi}\,e^{-i\pi(\nu+1)/2}}{\Gamma(-\nu)},
+ \qquad
+ M_W(\nu)=
+ \begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\pi\nu}&c_W(\nu)\\0&e^{i\pi\nu}\end{pmatrix},
+ \qquad \det M_W=1.
+ \label{eq:fedoryuk-weber-matrix}
+\end{equation}
+This is an exact model identity (DLMF 12.2.19), not yet the physical transfer
+matrix.  In a transported WKB basis the latter would have the schematic but
+unambiguous factorization
+\begin{equation}
+ M_{\rm glob}=B_{\rm out}M_A^{\chi_A}B_2M_WB_1G_0,
+ \qquad \chi_A\in\{0,1\},
+ \label{eq:fedoryuk-global-matrix-scheme}
+\end{equation}
+where \(M_A\) occurs only if the continuation is forced past the remaining
+simple point, the \(B_j\) are overlap and sheet-change matrices, and \(G_0\)
+is the origin gauge transition.  Their entries cannot be assigned before the
+paths and branches are constructed.  On a positive circuit the factor
+\(z^{-\delta}\) has multiplier \(e^{-2\pi i\delta}\); the transformed
+solution must therefore acquire \(e^{2\pi i\delta}\) for their product to be
+single-valued.
+
+\begin{conjecture}[Minimal Fedoryuk--Bargmann connection lemma]
+\label{conj:fedoryuk-bargmann-lemma}
+For fixed \(n\) and \(\delta\) in a compact real set, sufficiently small
+\(\varepsilon\) admits a finite, uniformly controlled chain of punctured
+origin, WKB, close-pair Weber and, if necessary, Airy domains.  It identifies
+the compensated analytic origin germ with the imaginary-axis Weber pair,
+realizes the reciprocal gauge monodromy, and makes the final enhanced-branch
+coefficient
+\begin{equation}
+ \mathcal C_{\rm Fed}(E,\eta,\delta)
+ =A(E,\varepsilon,\delta)
+ \left\{\Gamma[-\nu_{\rm eff}(E,\varepsilon,\delta)]^{-1}
+ +O(\varepsilon^3)\right\},
+ \label{eq:fedoryuk-conditional-coefficient}
+\end{equation}
+where \(A\) is bounded away from zero.  Its vanishing is equivalent, locally
+in energy, to absence of the enhanced essential branch and hence to the
+exact Bargmann entireness condition.  Moreover, \(\nu_{\rm eff}=n\) has the
+local expansion determined by
+\eqref{eq:fedoryuk-imaginary-branch} and
+\eqref{eq:fedoryuk-local-constant}.
+\end{conjecture}
+
+The missing assertion is global: Fedoryuk's verified local WKB, meromorphic,
+simple-turning-point and close-turning-point results do not themselves supply
+the univalent chain, its progressive paths at the fourth-order pole, uniform
+overlap errors, or the Bargmann identification.  Conditional on
+Conjecture~\ref{conj:fedoryuk-bargmann-lemma}, the simple zeros of
+\(1/\Gamma(-\nu)\) and a Rouch\'e argument yield
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ \mathcal C_{\rm Fed}(E,\eta,\delta)=0
+ \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+ \frac{E_n}{V}={}&-\frac32\eta^{4/3}\\
+ &+\left[\delta+\sqrt3\left(n+\frac12\right)-1\right]\eta^{2/3}\\
+ &+\frac{\delta(1-2\delta)}6-\frac{6n^2+6n+1}{72}
+ +O(\eta^{-2/3}).
+ \end{aligned}
+ \label{eq:fedoryuk-conditional-quantization}
+\end{equation}
+Thus the implication from the minimal lemma is proved, but the lemma is not.
+The positive-\(\sqrt3\) sign agrees independently with Bogoliubov and Olver,
+and the constant agrees with formal Rayleigh--Schr\"odinger theory; neither
+agreement proves the global connection.  In particular the known
+\(K_n(\delta)\eta^{-2/3}\) term in
+\eqref{eq:strong-drive-energy-constant} remains a formal operator
+coefficient, not a Fedoryuk consequence.  Full hypotheses and the proposed
+proof route are recorded in the companion global-connection audit.
+
